    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Colors of Things that Go</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> That's a Blue Bus.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Reading: Sequence pictures to demonstrate comprehension of texts that include a clear beginning, middle, and end. Reading: Show comprehension of the main action or event in a very simple storybook with pictures. Phonemic Awareness: Identify sounds of the letters of the alphabet when supported with a concrete item or picture representing that sound.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can sequence pictures to show a clear beginning, middle, and end of the action in a simple story (e.g., "Marta sees the red bus." "The red bus stops." "People exit the bus."). Can show comprehension of the main action or event in a simple storybook by pointing to pictures. Can identify sounds of the letters of the alphabet when supported with a concrete item or picture representing that sound (e.g., identifying the sound /y/ in the word "yellow" when shown a picture of a yellow taxi).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing large flashcards of vehicles (car, bus, bike, truck, train, taxi) and colors (red, blue, green, yellow, purple, orange). The teacher holds up a flashcard and says, "Look! A red car!" Students repeat chorally. Then, the teacher holds up a vehicle flashcard and a color flashcard and asks, "What color is the car?" expecting a one-word answer or pointing to the correct color. This reviews vocabulary from Lesson 1 and prepares students for reading about colors and vehicles.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to identify words. For example, the teacher points to the word "bus" written below a picture of a bus and says, "Bus!" Then, the teacher points to the word "blue" and says, "Blue." The teacher then points to "blue bus" and says, "Blue bus." The teacher emphasizes the initial sound of /b/ in "bus" and /k/ in "car" and /y/ in "yellow" by isolating the sound, "B-b-bus," "C-c-car," "Y-y-yellow," and asking students to repeat. The teacher demonstrates pointing to the correct picture when a word is read aloud.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses simple questions and gestures for 1st graders to check understanding of the words.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a picture of a red car) "Is this a blue car?" (Students shake heads or say "No" / "Red.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a picture of a yellow taxi) "What is this?" (Students point or say "Taxi" / "Yellow taxi.")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Colors of Things that Go"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple storybook or large flashcards with short, sequential sentences and corresponding pictures. The text focuses on vehicles and colors, forming a clear beginning, middle, and end (e.g., "Marta sees a red bus." "The red bus stops." "People exit the bus." or "A green car drives. A blue car stops."). The genre is a short narrative excerpt for early readers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Show comprehension of the main action or event in a very simple storybook with pictures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> The teacher reads the simple story/sentences aloud while pointing to the words and pictures. Students follow along and point to the pictures as the teacher reads the matching words. For example, "That's a blue bus." (students point to the blue bus).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Identify Main Action:</b> After reading, the teacher asks students to point to the picture that shows "the bus stops" or "the car drives."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sequencing:</b> The teacher displays 3-4 pictures from the story in mixed order. The teacher re-reads the story sentence by sentence, and students point to the picture that matches each sentence in sequence.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading sentences with Color + noun ("green car"), WH-questions ("What color is the car?"), and Present simple ("It is a red bike."). Identifying initial sounds of /k/, /b/, /y/ in familiar vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ol style="margin-bottom: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice: Picture-Sentence Matching:</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with 4-5 simple sentences (e.g., "It is a red bike.", "That is a yellow taxi.", "This is a blue bus.") and pictures of the corresponding vehicles in mixed order. Students draw a line from each sentence to the correct picture. The teacher circulates, providing support and checking accuracy.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice: Sequence the Story:</b> The teacher gives students a set of 3 sequenced pictures showing a simple action (e.g., a green truck driving, then stopping, then going again) and three corresponding short sentences written below (e.g., "A green truck drives.", "The truck stops.", "The truck goes."). Students read the sentences and cut and paste the pictures in the correct order to match the text. The teacher guides students to identify words like "truck" and "green" and the action verbs. The teacher also asks, "What sound does 'green' start with?" or "What sound does 'truck' start with?" for phonemic awareness.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction/Peer Check: "Point and Read":</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of flashcards with pictures of colored vehicles (e.g., blue bus, red car, yellow taxi, green truck). One student reads a simple sentence aloud (e.g., "Please, point to the red car."). The other student points to the correct picture. They switch roles after a few turns. This activity reinforces reading comprehension and polite requests.</li>
                </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Colors of Things that Go"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Picture Story Sequencing.</b> Students are given a new, very simple story (3-4 sentences long) about "Colors of Things that Go" with corresponding pictures printed separately. The story includes target vocabulary (e.g., "A big yellow taxi goes.", "It stops at the store.", "A small red bike rides next.") and grammatical structures. The text is very simple, suitable for 1st-grade reading. Each student receives a text and the jumbled pictures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ol style="margin-bottom: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the short story aloud once.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the story silently, pointing to words they know.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students then cut out the pictures and glue them onto a piece of paper in the correct sequence as described in the story.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">As they work, the teacher asks individuals, "What color is this taxi?" or "What letter sound is at the beginning of 'yellow'?" to assess comprehension and phonemic awareness.</li>
                </ol>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Students demonstrate their ability to read and comprehend the main actions and sequence events in a simple story. They actively use their reading skills to match text to visual information.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Observation Checklist & Individual Check.</b> The teacher uses a simple checklist to observe students' ability to sequence pictures and identify specific elements (colors, vehicles, actions) from the text. For individual checks, the teacher presents a picture and asks students to point to the corresponding word or identify the initial sound of the word.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task: "Read and Point":</b> The teacher gives each student a worksheet with 3-4 pictures of vehicles with different colors (e.g., blue bus, red car, green bike). The teacher reads a sentence aloud (e.g., "Point to the green bike."). Students must identify and point to the correct picture on their worksheet. Then, the teacher asks, "What color is this bus?" and "What sound does 'bus' start with?" (pointing to a picture of a bus).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly sequences pictures according to the story text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates comprehension of main action/event by pointing to pictures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies initial sounds of familiar vocabulary (e.g., /k/ in "car", /b/ in "bus", /y/ in "yellow").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses gestures or one-word answers appropriately when asked about colors/objects.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are given a simple drawing of a happy face and a sad face. They point to the happy face if they feel they can read and understand the stories about the vehicles and colors, or the sad face if it was difficult. Alternatively, the teacher asks, "Did you learn about the blue bus today?" and students give a thumbs up or thumbs down.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite part about reading today?" (Students can point to a picture of a vehicle or color).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or a little bit hard to put the pictures in order?" (Students show thumbs up for easy, thumbs to the side for a little hard).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students successfully matched sentences to pictures and sequenced the simple stories. The phonemic awareness activities helped reinforce initial sounds. Some students needed more individual support to identify specific words like "new" or "old." I will need to incorporate more explicit word recognition practice in the next lesson's warm-up to strengthen sight word recognition for early readers. The politeness norms (e.g., "Please, point to...") were integrated naturally, but I can encourage more student-led requests.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This reading lesson helped students recognize vehicles and colors in written form and understand simple actions. For Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will use these same vocabulary words and sentence structures ("It is a red bike.") to actively describe and ask about vehicles and colors with their peers, building on their comprehension by producing oral language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>